---
title: Column Definitions Guide
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This guide is about setting up column definitions for your table and NOT about the actual "},{"type":"link","href":"./columns","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column"}]},{"type":"text","value":" objects that are generated within the table instance."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column defs are the single most important part of building a table. They are responsible for:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Building the underlying data model that will be used for everything including sorting, filtering, grouping, etc."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Formatting the data model into what will be displayed in the table"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Creating "},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/index/interfaces/HeaderGroup_Core","children":[{"type":"text","value":"header groups"}]},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/index/interfaces/Header_Core","children":[{"type":"text","value":"headers"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and footers"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Creating columns for display-only purposes, e.g. action buttons, checkboxes, expanders, sparklines, etc."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"column-def-types","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Def Types"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The following \"types\" of column defs aren't actually TypeScript types, but more so a way to talk about and describe overall categories of column defs:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Accessor Columns"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Accessor columns have an underlying data model which means they can be sorted, filtered, grouped, etc."}]}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Display Columns"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Display columns do "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"not"}]},{"type":"text","value":" have a data model which means they cannot be sorted, filtered, etc, but they can be used to display arbitrary content in the table, e.g. a row actions button, checkbox, expander, etc."}]}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Grouping Columns"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Group columns do "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"not"}]},{"type":"text","value":" have a data model so they too cannot be sorted, filtered, etc, and are used to group other columns together. It's common to define a header or footer for a column group."}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"column-helpers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Helpers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"While column defs are just plain objects at the end of the day, a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createColumnHelper"},{"type":"text","value":" function is exposed from the table core which, when called with your features type and row type, returns a utility for creating different column definition types with the highest type-safety possible."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In v9, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createColumnHelper"},{"type":"text","value":" requires two type parameters: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":" (from your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"features"},{"type":"text","value":" object) and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TData"},{"type":"text","value":" (your row type). Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"typeof features"},{"type":"text","value":" to get the features type."}]},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHook"},{"type":"text","value":" factory, it returns a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createAppColumnHelper"},{"type":"text","value":" that is already bound to your features type, so you only pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TData"},{"type":"text","value":". See the "},{"type":"link","href":"../framework/react/guide/composable-tables","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Composable Tables Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here's an example of creating and using a column helper:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Define your row shape\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n  visits: number\n  status: string\n  progress: number\n}\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({}) // or tableFeatures({ rowSortingFeature, ... })\nconst columnHelper = createColumnHelper<typeof features, Person>()\n\n// Make some columns! Use columnHelper.columns([...]) for better type inference with nested groups\nconst defaultColumns = columnHelper.columns([\n  // Display Column\n  columnHelper.display({\n    id: 'actions',\n    cell: (props) => <RowActions row={props.row} />,\n  }),\n  // Grouping Column\n  columnHelper.group({\n    header: 'Name',\n    footer: (props) => props.column.id,\n    columns: [\n      // Accessor Column\n      columnHelper.accessor('firstName', {\n        cell: (info) => info.getValue(),\n        footer: (props) => props.column.id,\n      }),\n      // Accessor Column\n      columnHelper.accessor((row) => row.lastName, {\n        id: 'lastName',\n        cell: (info) => info.getValue(),\n        header: () => <span>Last Name</span>,\n        footer: (props) => props.column.id,\n      }),\n    ],\n  }),\n  // Grouping Column\n  columnHelper.group({\n    header: 'Info',\n    footer: (props) => props.column.id,\n    columns: [\n      // Accessor Column\n      columnHelper.accessor('age', {\n        header: () => 'Age',\n        footer: (props) => props.column.id,\n      }),\n      // Grouping Column\n      columnHelper.group({\n        header: 'More Info',\n        columns: [\n          // Accessor Column\n          columnHelper.accessor('visits', {\n            header: () => <span>Visits</span>,\n            footer: (props) => props.column.id,\n          }),\n          // Accessor Column\n          columnHelper.accessor('status', {\n            header: 'Status',\n            footer: (props) => props.column.id,\n          }),\n          // Accessor Column\n          columnHelper.accessor('progress', {\n            header: 'Profile Progress',\n            footer: (props) => props.column.id,\n          }),\n        ],\n      }),\n    ],\n  }),\n])","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"creating-accessor-columns","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Creating Accessor Columns"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Accessor columns are unique in that they must be configured to extract primitive values for each item in your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" array."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are 3 ways to do this:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If your items are "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"objects"},{"type":"text","value":", use an object-key that corresponds to the value you want to extract."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If your items are nested "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"arrays"},{"type":"text","value":", use an array index that corresponds to the value you want to extract."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use an accessor function that returns the value you want to extract."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"object-keys","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Object Keys"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If each of your items is an object with the following shape:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"type Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n  visits: number\n  status: string\n  progress: number\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You could extract the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"firstName"},{"type":"text","value":" value like so:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"\ncolumnHelper.accessor('firstName')\n\n// OR\n\n{\n  accessorKey: 'firstName',\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"deep-keys","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Deep Keys"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If each of your items is an object with the following shape:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"type Person = {\n  name: {\n    first: string\n    last: string\n  }\n  info: {\n    age: number\n    visits: number\n  }\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You could extract the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"first"},{"type":"text","value":" value like so:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"columnHelper.accessor('name.first', {\n  id: 'firstName',\n})\n\n// OR\n\n{\n  accessorKey: 'name.first',\n  id: 'firstName',\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"array-indices","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Array Indices"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If each of your items is an array with the following shape:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"type Sales = [Date, number]","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You could extract the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"number"},{"type":"text","value":" value like so:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"columnHelper.accessor(row => row[1], {\n  id: 'amount',\n})\n\n// OR\n\n{\n  accessorKey: '1', // string array index\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"accessorKey"},{"type":"text","value":" with array data, the key must be a string (e.g. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'1'"},{"type":"text","value":", not the number "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1"},{"type":"text","value":")."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"accessor-functions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Accessor Functions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If each of your items is an object with the following shape:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"type Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n  visits: number\n  status: string\n  progress: number\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You could extract a computed full-name value like so:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"columnHelper.accessor(row => `${row.firstName} ${row.lastName}`, {\n  id: 'fullName',\n})\n\n// OR\n\n{\n  id: 'fullName',\n  accessorFn: row => `${row.firstName} ${row.lastName}`,\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"🧠 Remember, the accessed value is what is used to sort, filter, etc. so you'll want to make sure your accessor function returns a primitive value that can be manipulated in a meaningful way. If you return a non-primitive value like an object or array, you will need the appropriate filter/sort/grouping functions to manipulate them, or even supply your own! 😬"}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"unique-column-ids","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Unique Column IDs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Columns are uniquely identified with 3 strategies:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If defining an accessor column with an object key or array index, the same will be used to uniquely identify the column."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Any periods ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"."},{"type":"text","value":") in an object key will be replaced by underscores ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"_"},{"type":"text","value":")."}]}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If defining an accessor column with an accessor function"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The column's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":" property will be used to uniquely identify the column OR"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If a primitive "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"string"},{"type":"text","value":" header is supplied, that header string will be used to uniquely identify the column"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"🧠 An easy way to remember: If you define a column with an accessor function, either provide a string header or provide a unique "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":" property."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"dynamic-column-definitions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dynamic Column Definitions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column definitions do not have to be hard-coded. When the shape of your data is not known ahead of time (arbitrary API responses, user-uploaded CSV files, user-configurable reports), you can generate the column definitions from the data itself at runtime."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"🧠 Naming note: accessor functions create computed (\"derived\") values within a known row shape, like the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fullName"},{"type":"text","value":" example above. Dynamic column definitions generate the column set itself when the row shape is unknown. The two techniques are complementary."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Since there is no static row type to infer from, type your rows as a generic record and build plain column def objects. The column helper offers no benefit here because there is no known shape for it to infer against:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"type DynamicRow = Record<string, unknown>\n\nconst columns: Array<ColumnDef<typeof features, DynamicRow>> = data.length\n  ? Object.keys(data[0]).map((key) => ({\n      accessorKey: key,\n      header: formatHeader(key), // e.g. 'firstName' -> 'First Name'\n      cell: (info) => String(info.getValue() ?? ''), // values are unknown, so coerce for rendering\n    }))\n  : []","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two things to keep in mind:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stable identity."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Rebuild the columns array only when the data actually changes (for example, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useMemo"},{"type":"text","value":" keyed on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" in React, or a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"computed"},{"type":"text","value":"/memo in other frameworks). Recreating column defs on every render forces the table to reprocess all of its columns."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Runtime type detection."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Since values are typed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unknown"},{"type":"text","value":", inspect a sample value per key to choose type-appropriate options: a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFn"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filterFn"},{"type":"text","value":" suited to numbers, dates, or booleans, and a matching filter UI. The registered function names described below make this a simple switch on the detected type."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the "},{"type":"link","href":"../framework/react/examples/basic-dynamic-columns","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dynamic Columns example"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (available for every framework adapter) for a complete implementation with data type detection, per-type sort and filter functions, and per-type filter components powered by column faceting."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"column-formatting-rendering","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Formatting & Rendering"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, column cells will display their data model value as a string. You can override this behavior by providing custom rendering implementations. Each implementation is provided with relevant information about the cell, header or footer and returns something your framework adapter can render e.g. JSX/Components/strings/etc. This will depend on which adapter you are using."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are a couple of formatters available to you:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell"},{"type":"text","value":": Used for formatting cells."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aggregatedCell"},{"type":"text","value":": Used for formatting cells when aggregated."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"header"},{"type":"text","value":": Used for formatting headers."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"footer"},{"type":"text","value":": Used for formatting footers."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"cell-formatting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cell Formatting"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can provide a custom cell formatter by passing a function to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell"},{"type":"text","value":" property and using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"props.getValue()"},{"type":"text","value":" function to access your cell's value:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"columnHelper.accessor('firstName', {\n  cell: (props) => <span>{props.getValue().toUpperCase()}</span>,\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cell formatters are also provided the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"row"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table"},{"type":"text","value":" objects, so you can customize the cell formatting beyond just the cell value. The example below provides "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"firstName"},{"type":"text","value":" as the accessor, but also displays a prefixed user ID located on the original row object:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"columnHelper.accessor('firstName', {\n  cell: (props) => (\n    <span>{`${props.row.original.id} - ${props.getValue()}`}</span>\n  ),\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"aggregated-cell-formatting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Aggregated Cell Formatting"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Aggregated cells render in place of regular cells when rows are grouped. This is discussed in more detail in the "},{"type":"link","href":"../framework/react/guide/grouping","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grouping Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":", which is available for every framework adapter."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"header-footer-formatting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Header & Footer Formatting"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Headers and footers do not have access to row data, but still use the same concepts for displaying custom content."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"feature-options-on-column-defs","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Feature Options on Column Defs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Beyond accessors and formatters, column defs carry per-column options for whichever features your table has enabled. Common examples include "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFn"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filterFn"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aggregationFn"},{"type":"text","value":", and enable flags such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enableSorting"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enableColumnFilter"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"columnHelper.accessor('age', {\n  sortFn: 'basic', // a name typed from the sortFns registry in tableFeatures\n  filterFn: 'inNumberRange', // a name typed from the filterFns registry\n  sortDescFirst: true,\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In v9, the string names you can pass to these options are typed from the function registries you include in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":" (such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFns"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filterFns"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aggregationFns"},{"type":"text","value":"). Passing a name that is not in the registry is a type error. Custom functions can be provided inline or registered under your own names. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./helpers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type Helpers Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for how registries shape these types, and each feature guide for the full set of available options."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"column-meta","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Meta"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column defs also accept a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"meta"},{"type":"text","value":" property for attaching your own arbitrary, strongly-typed data to a column (for example, a filter variant or a detected data type). See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./table-and-column-meta","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table and Column Meta Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for details on typing it with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"metaHelper"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"column-def-types","text":"Column Def Types","level":2},{"id":"column-helpers","text":"Column Helpers","level":2},{"id":"creating-accessor-columns","text":"Creating Accessor Columns","level":2},{"id":"object-keys","text":"Object Keys","level":3},{"id":"deep-keys","text":"Deep Keys","level":3},{"id":"array-indices","text":"Array Indices","level":3},{"id":"accessor-functions","text":"Accessor Functions","level":3},{"id":"unique-column-ids","text":"Unique Column IDs","level":2},{"id":"dynamic-column-definitions","text":"Dynamic Column Definitions","level":2},{"id":"column-formatting-rendering","text":"Column Formatting & Rendering","level":2},{"id":"cell-formatting","text":"Cell Formatting","level":3},{"id":"aggregated-cell-formatting","text":"Aggregated Cell Formatting","level":3},{"id":"header-footer-formatting","text":"Header & Footer Formatting","level":3},{"id":"feature-options-on-column-defs","text":"Feature Options on Column Defs","level":2},{"id":"column-meta","text":"Column Meta","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n> [!NOTE]\n> This guide is about setting up column definitions for your table and NOT about the actual [`column`](./columns) objects that are generated within the table instance.\n\nColumn defs are the single most important part of building a table. They are responsible for:\n\n- Building the underlying data model that will be used for everything including sorting, filtering, grouping, etc.\n- Formatting the data model into what will be displayed in the table\n- Creating [header groups](../reference/index/interfaces/HeaderGroup_Core), [headers](../reference/index/interfaces/Header_Core) and footers\n- Creating columns for display-only purposes, e.g. action buttons, checkboxes, expanders, sparklines, etc.\n\n## Column Def Types\n\nThe following \"types\" of column defs aren't actually TypeScript types, but more so a way to talk about and describe overall categories of column defs:\n\n- `Accessor Columns`\n  - Accessor columns have an underlying data model which means they can be sorted, filtered, grouped, etc.\n- `Display Columns`\n  - Display columns do **not** have a data model which means they cannot be sorted, filtered, etc, but they can be used to display arbitrary content in the table, e.g. a row actions button, checkbox, expander, etc.\n- `Grouping Columns`\n  - Group columns do **not** have a data model so they too cannot be sorted, filtered, etc, and are used to group other columns together. It's common to define a header or footer for a column group.\n\n## Column Helpers\n\nWhile column defs are just plain objects at the end of the day, a `createColumnHelper` function is exposed from the table core which, when called with your features type and row type, returns a utility for creating different column definition types with the highest type-safety possible.\n\nIn v9, `createColumnHelper` requires two type parameters: `TFeatures` (from your `features` object) and `TData` (your row type). Use `typeof features` to get the features type.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> If you use the `createTableHook` factory, it returns a `createAppColumnHelper` that is already bound to your features type, so you only pass `TData`. See the [Composable Tables Guide](../framework/react/guide/composable-tables).\n\nHere's an example of creating and using a column helper:\n\n```tsx\n// Define your row shape\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n  visits: number\n  status: string\n  progress: number\n}\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({}) // or tableFeatures({ rowSortingFeature, ... })\nconst columnHelper = createColumnHelper<typeof features, Person>()\n\n// Make some columns! Use columnHelper.columns([...]) for better type inference with nested groups\nconst defaultColumns = columnHelper.columns([\n  // Display Column\n  columnHelper.display({\n    id: 'actions',\n    cell: (props) => <RowActions row={props.row} />,\n  }),\n  // Grouping Column\n  columnHelper.group({\n    header: 'Name',\n    footer: (props) => props.column.id,\n    columns: [\n      // Accessor Column\n      columnHelper.accessor('firstName', {\n        cell: (info) => info.getValue(),\n        footer: (props) => props.column.id,\n      }),\n      // Accessor Column\n      columnHelper.accessor((row) => row.lastName, {\n        id: 'lastName',\n        cell: (info) => info.getValue(),\n        header: () => <span>Last Name</span>,\n        footer: (props) => props.column.id,\n      }),\n    ],\n  }),\n  // Grouping Column\n  columnHelper.group({\n    header: 'Info',\n    footer: (props) => props.column.id,\n    columns: [\n      // Accessor Column\n      columnHelper.accessor('age', {\n        header: () => 'Age',\n        footer: (props) => props.column.id,\n      }),\n      // Grouping Column\n      columnHelper.group({\n        header: 'More Info',\n        columns: [\n          // Accessor Column\n          columnHelper.accessor('visits', {\n            header: () => <span>Visits</span>,\n            footer: (props) => props.column.id,\n          }),\n          // Accessor Column\n          columnHelper.accessor('status', {\n            header: 'Status',\n            footer: (props) => props.column.id,\n          }),\n          // Accessor Column\n          columnHelper.accessor('progress', {\n            header: 'Profile Progress',\n            footer: (props) => props.column.id,\n          }),\n        ],\n      }),\n    ],\n  }),\n])\n```\n\n## Creating Accessor Columns\n\nAccessor columns are unique in that they must be configured to extract primitive values for each item in your `data` array.\n\nThere are 3 ways to do this:\n\n- If your items are `objects`, use an object-key that corresponds to the value you want to extract.\n- If your items are nested `arrays`, use an array index that corresponds to the value you want to extract.\n- Use an accessor function that returns the value you want to extract.\n\n### Object Keys\n\nIf each of your items is an object with the following shape:\n\n```tsx\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n  visits: number\n  status: string\n  progress: number\n}\n```\n\nYou could extract the `firstName` value like so:\n\n```tsx\n\ncolumnHelper.accessor('firstName')\n\n// OR\n\n{\n  accessorKey: 'firstName',\n}\n```\n\n### Deep Keys\n\nIf each of your items is an object with the following shape:\n\n```tsx\ntype Person = {\n  name: {\n    first: string\n    last: string\n  }\n  info: {\n    age: number\n    visits: number\n  }\n}\n```\n\nYou could extract the `first` value like so:\n\n```tsx\ncolumnHelper.accessor('name.first', {\n  id: 'firstName',\n})\n\n// OR\n\n{\n  accessorKey: 'name.first',\n  id: 'firstName',\n}\n```\n\n### Array Indices\n\nIf each of your items is an array with the following shape:\n\n```tsx\ntype Sales = [Date, number]\n```\n\nYou could extract the `number` value like so:\n\n```tsx\ncolumnHelper.accessor(row => row[1], {\n  id: 'amount',\n})\n\n// OR\n\n{\n  accessorKey: '1', // string array index\n}\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> When using `accessorKey` with array data, the key must be a string (e.g. `'1'`, not the number `1`).\n\n### Accessor Functions\n\nIf each of your items is an object with the following shape:\n\n```tsx\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n  visits: number\n  status: string\n  progress: number\n}\n```\n\nYou could extract a computed full-name value like so:\n\n```tsx\ncolumnHelper.accessor(row => `${row.firstName} ${row.lastName}`, {\n  id: 'fullName',\n})\n\n// OR\n\n{\n  id: 'fullName',\n  accessorFn: row => `${row.firstName} ${row.lastName}`,\n}\n```\n\n> 🧠 Remember, the accessed value is what is used to sort, filter, etc. so you'll want to make sure your accessor function returns a primitive value that can be manipulated in a meaningful way. If you return a non-primitive value like an object or array, you will need the appropriate filter/sort/grouping functions to manipulate them, or even supply your own! 😬\n\n## Unique Column IDs\n\nColumns are uniquely identified with 3 strategies:\n\n- If defining an accessor column with an object key or array index, the same will be used to uniquely identify the column.\n  - Any periods (`.`) in an object key will be replaced by underscores (`_`).\n- If defining an accessor column with an accessor function\n  - The column's `id` property will be used to uniquely identify the column OR\n  - If a primitive `string` header is supplied, that header string will be used to uniquely identify the column\n\n> 🧠 An easy way to remember: If you define a column with an accessor function, either provide a string header or provide a unique `id` property.\n\n## Dynamic Column Definitions\n\nColumn definitions do not have to be hard-coded. When the shape of your data is not known ahead of time (arbitrary API responses, user-uploaded CSV files, user-configurable reports), you can generate the column definitions from the data itself at runtime.\n\n> 🧠 Naming note: accessor functions create computed (\"derived\") values within a known row shape, like the `fullName` example above. Dynamic column definitions generate the column set itself when the row shape is unknown. The two techniques are complementary.\n\nSince there is no static row type to infer from, type your rows as a generic record and build plain column def objects. The column helper offers no benefit here because there is no known shape for it to infer against:\n\n```tsx\ntype DynamicRow = Record<string, unknown>\n\nconst columns: Array<ColumnDef<typeof features, DynamicRow>> = data.length\n  ? Object.keys(data[0]).map((key) => ({\n      accessorKey: key,\n      header: formatHeader(key), // e.g. 'firstName' -> 'First Name'\n      cell: (info) => String(info.getValue() ?? ''), // values are unknown, so coerce for rendering\n    }))\n  : []\n```\n\nTwo things to keep in mind:\n\n- **Stable identity.** Rebuild the columns array only when the data actually changes (for example, `useMemo` keyed on `data` in React, or a `computed`/memo in other frameworks). Recreating column defs on every render forces the table to reprocess all of its columns.\n- **Runtime type detection.** Since values are typed `unknown`, inspect a sample value per key to choose type-appropriate options: a `sortFn` and `filterFn` suited to numbers, dates, or booleans, and a matching filter UI. The registered function names described below make this a simple switch on the detected type.\n\nSee the [Dynamic Columns example](../framework/react/examples/basic-dynamic-columns) (available for every framework adapter) for a complete implementation with data type detection, per-type sort and filter functions, and per-type filter components powered by column faceting.\n\n## Column Formatting & Rendering\n\nBy default, column cells will display their data model value as a string. You can override this behavior by providing custom rendering implementations. Each implementation is provided with relevant information about the cell, header or footer and returns something your framework adapter can render e.g. JSX/Components/strings/etc. This will depend on which adapter you are using.\n\nThere are a couple of formatters available to you:\n\n- `cell`: Used for formatting cells.\n- `aggregatedCell`: Used for formatting cells when aggregated.\n- `header`: Used for formatting headers.\n- `footer`: Used for formatting footers.\n\n### Cell Formatting\n\nYou can provide a custom cell formatter by passing a function to the `cell` property and using the `props.getValue()` function to access your cell's value:\n\n```tsx\ncolumnHelper.accessor('firstName', {\n  cell: (props) => <span>{props.getValue().toUpperCase()}</span>,\n})\n```\n\nCell formatters are also provided the `row` and `table` objects, so you can customize the cell formatting beyond just the cell value. The example below provides `firstName` as the accessor, but also displays a prefixed user ID located on the original row object:\n\n```tsx\ncolumnHelper.accessor('firstName', {\n  cell: (props) => (\n    <span>{`${props.row.original.id} - ${props.getValue()}`}</span>\n  ),\n})\n```\n\n### Aggregated Cell Formatting\n\nAggregated cells render in place of regular cells when rows are grouped. This is discussed in more detail in the [Grouping Guide](../framework/react/guide/grouping), which is available for every framework adapter.\n\n### Header & Footer Formatting\n\nHeaders and footers do not have access to row data, but still use the same concepts for displaying custom content.\n\n## Feature Options on Column Defs\n\nBeyond accessors and formatters, column defs carry per-column options for whichever features your table has enabled. Common examples include `sortFn`, `filterFn`, `aggregationFn`, and enable flags such as `enableSorting` or `enableColumnFilter`.\n\n```tsx\ncolumnHelper.accessor('age', {\n  sortFn: 'basic', // a name typed from the sortFns registry in tableFeatures\n  filterFn: 'inNumberRange', // a name typed from the filterFns registry\n  sortDescFirst: true,\n})\n```\n\nIn v9, the string names you can pass to these options are typed from the function registries you include in `tableFeatures` (such as `sortFns`, `filterFns`, and `aggregationFns`). Passing a name that is not in the registry is a type error. Custom functions can be provided inline or registered under your own names. See the [Type Helpers Guide](./helpers) for how registries shape these types, and each feature guide for the full set of available options.\n\n## Column Meta\n\nColumn defs also accept a `meta` property for attaching your own arbitrary, strongly-typed data to a column (for example, a filter variant or a detected data type). See the [Table and Column Meta Guide](./table-and-column-meta) for details on typing it with `metaHelper`.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Column Definitions Guide\n---\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> This guide is about setting up column definitions for your table and NOT about the actual [`column`](./columns) objects that are generated within the table instance.\n\nColumn defs are the single most important part of building a table. They are responsible for:\n\n- Building the underlying data model that will be used for everything including sorting, filtering, grouping, etc.\n- Formatting the data model into what will be displayed in the table\n- Creating [header groups](../reference/index/interfaces/HeaderGroup_Core), [headers](../reference/index/interfaces/Header_Core) and footers\n- Creating columns for display-only purposes, e.g. action buttons, checkboxes, expanders, sparklines, etc.\n\n## Column Def Types\n\nThe following \"types\" of column defs aren't actually TypeScript types, but more so a way to talk about and describe overall categories of column defs:\n\n- `Accessor Columns`\n  - Accessor columns have an underlying data model which means they can be sorted, filtered, grouped, etc.\n- `Display Columns`\n  - Display columns do **not** have a data model which means they cannot be sorted, filtered, etc, but they can be used to display arbitrary content in the table, e.g. a row actions button, checkbox, expander, etc.\n- `Grouping Columns`\n  - Group columns do **not** have a data model so they too cannot be sorted, filtered, etc, and are used to group other columns together. It's common to define a header or footer for a column group.\n\n## Column Helpers\n\nWhile column defs are just plain objects at the end of the day, a `createColumnHelper` function is exposed from the table core which, when called with your features type and row type, returns a utility for creating different column definition types with the highest type-safety possible.\n\nIn v9, `createColumnHelper` requires two type parameters: `TFeatures` (from your `features` object) and `TData` (your row type). Use `typeof features` to get the features type.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> If you use the `createTableHook` factory, it returns a `createAppColumnHelper` that is already bound to your features type, so you only pass `TData`. See the [Composable Tables Guide](../framework/react/guide/composable-tables).\n\nHere's an example of creating and using a column helper:\n\n```tsx\n// Define your row shape\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n  visits: number\n  status: string\n  progress: number\n}\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({}) // or tableFeatures({ rowSortingFeature, ... })\nconst columnHelper = createColumnHelper<typeof features, Person>()\n\n// Make some columns! Use columnHelper.columns([...]) for better type inference with nested groups\nconst defaultColumns = columnHelper.columns([\n  // Display Column\n  columnHelper.display({\n    id: 'actions',\n    cell: (props) => <RowActions row={props.row} />,\n  }),\n  // Grouping Column\n  columnHelper.group({\n    header: 'Name',\n    footer: (props) => props.column.id,\n    columns: [\n      // Accessor Column\n      columnHelper.accessor('firstName', {\n        cell: (info) => info.getValue(),\n        footer: (props) => props.column.id,\n      }),\n      // Accessor Column\n      columnHelper.accessor((row) => row.lastName, {\n        id: 'lastName',\n        cell: (info) => info.getValue(),\n        header: () => <span>Last Name</span>,\n        footer: (props) => props.column.id,\n      }),\n    ],\n  }),\n  // Grouping Column\n  columnHelper.group({\n    header: 'Info',\n    footer: (props) => props.column.id,\n    columns: [\n      // Accessor Column\n      columnHelper.accessor('age', {\n        header: () => 'Age',\n        footer: (props) => props.column.id,\n      }),\n      // Grouping Column\n      columnHelper.group({\n        header: 'More Info',\n        columns: [\n          // Accessor Column\n          columnHelper.accessor('visits', {\n            header: () => <span>Visits</span>,\n            footer: (props) => props.column.id,\n          }),\n          // Accessor Column\n          columnHelper.accessor('status', {\n            header: 'Status',\n            footer: (props) => props.column.id,\n          }),\n          // Accessor Column\n          columnHelper.accessor('progress', {\n            header: 'Profile Progress',\n            footer: (props) => props.column.id,\n          }),\n        ],\n      }),\n    ],\n  }),\n])\n```\n\n## Creating Accessor Columns\n\nAccessor columns are unique in that they must be configured to extract primitive values for each item in your `data` array.\n\nThere are 3 ways to do this:\n\n- If your items are `objects`, use an object-key that corresponds to the value you want to extract.\n- If your items are nested `arrays`, use an array index that corresponds to the value you want to extract.\n- Use an accessor function that returns the value you want to extract.\n\n### Object Keys\n\nIf each of your items is an object with the following shape:\n\n```tsx\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n  visits: number\n  status: string\n  progress: number\n}\n```\n\nYou could extract the `firstName` value like so:\n\n```tsx\n\ncolumnHelper.accessor('firstName')\n\n// OR\n\n{\n  accessorKey: 'firstName',\n}\n```\n\n### Deep Keys\n\nIf each of your items is an object with the following shape:\n\n```tsx\ntype Person = {\n  name: {\n    first: string\n    last: string\n  }\n  info: {\n    age: number\n    visits: number\n  }\n}\n```\n\nYou could extract the `first` value like so:\n\n```tsx\ncolumnHelper.accessor('name.first', {\n  id: 'firstName',\n})\n\n// OR\n\n{\n  accessorKey: 'name.first',\n  id: 'firstName',\n}\n```\n\n### Array Indices\n\nIf each of your items is an array with the following shape:\n\n```tsx\ntype Sales = [Date, number]\n```\n\nYou could extract the `number` value like so:\n\n```tsx\ncolumnHelper.accessor(row => row[1], {\n  id: 'amount',\n})\n\n// OR\n\n{\n  accessorKey: '1', // string array index\n}\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> When using `accessorKey` with array data, the key must be a string (e.g. `'1'`, not the number `1`).\n\n### Accessor Functions\n\nIf each of your items is an object with the following shape:\n\n```tsx\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n  visits: number\n  status: string\n  progress: number\n}\n```\n\nYou could extract a computed full-name value like so:\n\n```tsx\ncolumnHelper.accessor(row => `${row.firstName} ${row.lastName}`, {\n  id: 'fullName',\n})\n\n// OR\n\n{\n  id: 'fullName',\n  accessorFn: row => `${row.firstName} ${row.lastName}`,\n}\n```\n\n> 🧠 Remember, the accessed value is what is used to sort, filter, etc. so you'll want to make sure your accessor function returns a primitive value that can be manipulated in a meaningful way. If you return a non-primitive value like an object or array, you will need the appropriate filter/sort/grouping functions to manipulate them, or even supply your own! 😬\n\n## Unique Column IDs\n\nColumns are uniquely identified with 3 strategies:\n\n- If defining an accessor column with an object key or array index, the same will be used to uniquely identify the column.\n  - Any periods (`.`) in an object key will be replaced by underscores (`_`).\n- If defining an accessor column with an accessor function\n  - The column's `id` property will be used to uniquely identify the column OR\n  - If a primitive `string` header is supplied, that header string will be used to uniquely identify the column\n\n> 🧠 An easy way to remember: If you define a column with an accessor function, either provide a string header or provide a unique `id` property.\n\n## Dynamic Column Definitions\n\nColumn definitions do not have to be hard-coded. When the shape of your data is not known ahead of time (arbitrary API responses, user-uploaded CSV files, user-configurable reports), you can generate the column definitions from the data itself at runtime.\n\n> 🧠 Naming note: accessor functions create computed (\"derived\") values within a known row shape, like the `fullName` example above. Dynamic column definitions generate the column set itself when the row shape is unknown. The two techniques are complementary.\n\nSince there is no static row type to infer from, type your rows as a generic record and build plain column def objects. The column helper offers no benefit here because there is no known shape for it to infer against:\n\n```tsx\ntype DynamicRow = Record<string, unknown>\n\nconst columns: Array<ColumnDef<typeof features, DynamicRow>> = data.length\n  ? Object.keys(data[0]).map((key) => ({\n      accessorKey: key,\n      header: formatHeader(key), // e.g. 'firstName' -> 'First Name'\n      cell: (info) => String(info.getValue() ?? ''), // values are unknown, so coerce for rendering\n    }))\n  : []\n```\n\nTwo things to keep in mind:\n\n- **Stable identity.** Rebuild the columns array only when the data actually changes (for example, `useMemo` keyed on `data` in React, or a `computed`/memo in other frameworks). Recreating column defs on every render forces the table to reprocess all of its columns.\n- **Runtime type detection.** Since values are typed `unknown`, inspect a sample value per key to choose type-appropriate options: a `sortFn` and `filterFn` suited to numbers, dates, or booleans, and a matching filter UI. The registered function names described below make this a simple switch on the detected type.\n\nSee the [Dynamic Columns example](../framework/react/examples/basic-dynamic-columns) (available for every framework adapter) for a complete implementation with data type detection, per-type sort and filter functions, and per-type filter components powered by column faceting.\n\n## Column Formatting & Rendering\n\nBy default, column cells will display their data model value as a string. You can override this behavior by providing custom rendering implementations. Each implementation is provided with relevant information about the cell, header or footer and returns something your framework adapter can render e.g. JSX/Components/strings/etc. This will depend on which adapter you are using.\n\nThere are a couple of formatters available to you:\n\n- `cell`: Used for formatting cells.\n- `aggregatedCell`: Used for formatting cells when aggregated.\n- `header`: Used for formatting headers.\n- `footer`: Used for formatting footers.\n\n### Cell Formatting\n\nYou can provide a custom cell formatter by passing a function to the `cell` property and using the `props.getValue()` function to access your cell's value:\n\n```tsx\ncolumnHelper.accessor('firstName', {\n  cell: (props) => <span>{props.getValue().toUpperCase()}</span>,\n})\n```\n\nCell formatters are also provided the `row` and `table` objects, so you can customize the cell formatting beyond just the cell value. The example below provides `firstName` as the accessor, but also displays a prefixed user ID located on the original row object:\n\n```tsx\ncolumnHelper.accessor('firstName', {\n  cell: (props) => (\n    <span>{`${props.row.original.id} - ${props.getValue()}`}</span>\n  ),\n})\n```\n\n### Aggregated Cell Formatting\n\nAggregated cells render in place of regular cells when rows are grouped. This is discussed in more detail in the [Grouping Guide](../framework/react/guide/grouping), which is available for every framework adapter.\n\n### Header & Footer Formatting\n\nHeaders and footers do not have access to row data, but still use the same concepts for displaying custom content.\n\n## Feature Options on Column Defs\n\nBeyond accessors and formatters, column defs carry per-column options for whichever features your table has enabled. Common examples include `sortFn`, `filterFn`, `aggregationFn`, and enable flags such as `enableSorting` or `enableColumnFilter`.\n\n```tsx\ncolumnHelper.accessor('age', {\n  sortFn: 'basic', // a name typed from the sortFns registry in tableFeatures\n  filterFn: 'inNumberRange', // a name typed from the filterFns registry\n  sortDescFirst: true,\n})\n```\n\nIn v9, the string names you can pass to these options are typed from the function registries you include in `tableFeatures` (such as `sortFns`, `filterFns`, and `aggregationFns`). Passing a name that is not in the registry is a type error. Custom functions can be provided inline or registered under your own names. See the [Type Helpers Guide](./helpers) for how registries shape these types, and each feature guide for the full set of available options.\n\n## Column Meta\n\nColumn defs also accept a `meta` property for attaching your own arbitrary, strongly-typed data to a column (for example, a filter variant or a detected data type). See the [Table and Column Meta Guide](./table-and-column-meta) for details on typing it with `metaHelper`.\n"}
